===Verb===
{{en-verb|+|+,~ing}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To grow aged; to become old or older; to show marks of age.
#: {{ux|en|He grew fat as he '''aged'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1824|author=Walter Savage Landor|title=Imaginary Conversations|passage=I am '''aging'''; that is, I have a whitish, or rather a light-coloured, hair here and there. Sober thinking brings them}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Joe Malarkey|chapter=Soap Operas|title=It’s Not the Fall That Kills You: Laughing All the Way to That Sudden Stop at the End|publisher=Latent Print|pages=123–124|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/itsnotfallthatki0000joem/page/124/mode/1up|isbn=1-892447-09-6|passage=However, if you have the misfortune of being a child actor born on the show, watch out. {{...}} The point is these kids '''age''' quickly.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2013|month=July–August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/stephen-p-lownie Stephen P. Lownie]; [http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/david-m-pelz David M. Pelz]|title=Stents to Prevent Stroke|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/stents-to-prevent-stroke|magazine=w:American Scientist|passage=As we '''age''', the major arteries of our bodies frequently become thickened with plaque, a fatty material with an oatmeal-like consistency that builds up along the inner lining of blood vessels. The reason plaque forms isn’t entirely known, but it seems to be related to high levels of cholesterol inducing an inflammatory response, which can also attract and trap more cellular debris over time.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170529010543/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/stents-to-prevent-stroke|archivedate=29 May 2017}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=12 October 2013|author=Elizabeth Hunter|chapter=9|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=j0jJEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT142|title=The Scribe|series=Irin Chronicles|seriesvolume=1|publisher=Recurve Press|isbn=978-0-9885205-9-2|passage=“Most of the scribes here came after the Rending. Many of them stopped casting the spells that prolong their life, so they are '''aging'''. More slowly than humans, but still '''aging'''.” / “How old are you?” / “Biologically?” He smiled. “Around thirty. But I’ve lived for over four hundred years.”}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|of a statement, prediction, etc.}} To suffer the passage of time so as to later be [[view]]ed or [[turn out]] in a certain way.
##: {{ux|en|His prediction that we didn't stand a chance hasn't '''aged''' well, now that we've won the cup.}}
##: {{ux|en|The sitcom was made in the 1970s and its casual sexism has not '''aged''' well.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to grow old; to impart the characteristics of age to.
#: {{ux|en|Grief '''ages''' us.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=To look at the hair by itself you'd say it was actually quite pretty, but on her head the gray sure '''ages''' her.|title=Aluhana|work=The Antioch Review|year=1998|month=Fall|author=Mare Freed|issue=4|volume=56}}
## To allow to mature.
##: {{ux|en|We '''age''' the whiskey for five years.}}
## To treat or tamper with in order to give a false appearance of age.
##: {{ux|en|This clock is modern, but it has been deliberately '''aged''' in an attempt to make it seem antique.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To determine the age of (the length of time that something has been alive or in existence).
#: {{ux|en|There are several ways to '''age''' trees.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[indicate]] or [[reveal]] that (a person) has been alive for a certain period of time, especially a long one.
#: {{ux|en|I clearly remember hearing the news of Kennedy's assassination. That '''ages''' me.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|title=This Week with David Brinkley|text=Mr. [David] Brinkley started out with network news. We got our news- I think it was the Huntley-Brinkley Report. I'm probably '''aging''' myself now, okay?|date=1992-06-14|role=[James?] Carville|medium=television production|network=ABC}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To allow (something) to persist by postponing an action that would extinguish it, as a debt.
